For the flat unary case: materialise boundary In<Tile<..>> inputs after the wrapper prelude:

  x = Tile { tensor: load(x_ptr.add_offsets(offsets), Some(in_bounds), ...), mask: Some(in_bounds) }

offsets / in_bounds / BLOCK_SIZE / n_elements come from the schedule-selected grid (see teenygrad-1nr.19 comment), not re-derived per member op.

description:
Not an OpenAI endpoint but a helper every model trait needs: encode text to token ids, decode ids to text (incrementally, for streaming, handling tokens that split a UTF-8 character), count tokens, list special tokens (BOS/EOS, chat-template markers) and the vocabulary size.

Uses:
- usage accounting (prompt and completion tokens) in every model trait;
- context checks in validate(): prompt plus max_completion_tokens against Capabilities::context_length, before generation starts;
- logit_bias (token ids) and logprobs (token text and bytes) in ChatModel and CompletionModel;
- token-id prompts accepted by EmbeddingModel and CompletionModel;
- a token-counting endpoint in the server, if wanted.

Design: object-safe Send + Sync trait, with an implementation over the tokenizers crate (Hugging Face tokenizer.json) that FunctionGemma's chat.rs already uses; models expose their tokenizer (e.g. a fn tokenizer(&self) -> Option<&dyn Tokenizer> on the model traits) so frontends and validation can count without generating. A whitespace tokenizer for the echo models, so their counts go through the same path. test_-prefixed unit tests, including incremental decoding across a split character.

DSpark (DeepSeek-AI, arXiv 2607.05147; training code at github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepSpec) on top of the speculative decoding infrastructure of teenygrad-35vc.12.

Drafter: a small DFlash-style model. Input: the anchor token plus gamma-1 mask tokens, which give gamma logits in one parallel pass. Hidden states from several target layers are concatenated, projected and RMSNorm'd, then injected as extra keys/values in every draft layer. It shares the target's frozen embedding and LM head. A low-rank Markov head (r=256) adds a bias from the previous draft token, sampled left to right. Block size gamma is about 5-7.

Verification: chain drafts with standard speculative sampling (accept min(1, p_t/p_d), truncate at the first rejection). Rejected positions roll back with KvCache::truncate (teeny-kvcache).

Scheduler: a confidence head estimates per-position acceptance; prefix survival is the product of the confidences, calibrated per position by temperature scaling. Verify lengths are chosen across the whole batch to maximise expected accepted tokens times the engine's profiled steps/s at the resulting batch token count. A throughput profile SPS(B) is measured at startup. Under load this shrinks drafts automatically, so it subsumes 35vc.12's 'disable adaptively at high batch'.

Work:
- target steps expose the chosen layers' hidden states as outputs;
- a drafter step graph plus a Markov/confidence-head loader;
- multi-token verify steps (35vc.4);
- the scheduler, run in the batching scheduler (35vc.9);
- SPS profiling in teeny-bench;
- acceptance length and tok/s in Timings.

Drafters are trained per target model. The paper trains them for Qwen3-4B/8B/14B and Gemma4-12B, and releases checkpoints for DeepSeek-V4 (check the HF repo for which others are public). So this needs a generic model builder (35vc.6) for a target with a drafter, or training one with DeepSpec, for example for FunctionGemma.

Check: the same output distribution as plain sampling (greedy: identical tokens); accepted length and per-user tok/s reported by teeny-bench, compared with no drafting and with n-gram drafting.

Reported: accepted length +27-31% over EAGLE-3 and +16-18% over DFlash on Qwen3; +57-85% per-user speed over MTP-1 in DeepSeek production at matched throughput.

There is no benchmark suite for the kernels the models depend on. FunctionGemma's branch carried an ad-hoc examples/matmul_bench.rs (timing Linear4Op at the decode and prompt-chunk shapes, over block sizes and the rows/reuse program orderings, after checking outputs against the host); it was dropped from teeny-llm-core in #169 because a benchmark does not belong in a model crate.

Add proper benchmarks for the critical kernels, at least: matmul/gemm and teeny_llm_core::kernels::linear::Linear4Op, GqaAttentionOp (with past length and sliding window), RMSNorm/LayerNorm, softmax, RoPE. Requirements:
- live alongside the kernels (teeny-kernels benches or a dedicated bench crate), not in an app crate;
- run on CUDA and on RISC-V (qemu for compiling the kernel cache, then on a board, e.g. a SpacemiT K3 via TEENY_RISCV_THREADS / TEENY_RISCV_AI_THREADS);
- parametrised by shape and block size so model shapes (e.g. FunctionGemma lm_head, gate/up) can be swept;
- verify a result against a host reference before timing;
- documented reference numbers so regressions are visible.

Reference point from the dropped bench, K3 8 X100 cores, block 16: lm_head ~41 ms, gate/up x64 ~12 ms (rows) / 8.6 ms (reuse). The old code is on feature/functiongemma at models/teeny-gemma/examples/matmul_bench.rs.
